> Can you explain why I need to include the mapillary.js script in the index.html file? I noticed I had to do this for the leaflet.js file as well.  Why isn't it enough for me to import the mapillary-js module?

`npm install mapillary-js` installs the package for Node/tooling, but it does not automatically send that package to the browser.

Your browser only knows how to load:

- JavaScript files referenced by `<script>` tags
- JavaScript modules referenced by URLs
- Bundled files produced by tools such as Vite or Webpack

This import:

```js
import { Viewer } from "mapillary-js";
```

uses a bare module name. Node understands how to resolve `"mapillary-js"` from `node_modules`, but browsers do not. A browser would need a URL, such as:

```js
import { Viewer } from "https://unpkg.com/mapillary-js@4.1.2/dist/mapillary.js";
```

and your script would need to be loaded as a module:

```html
<script type="module" src="js/streetview.js"></script>
```

Leaflet works similarly. The CDN script:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
```

downloads Leaflet and creates the global `L` object, which your code then uses:

```js
L.map("guessMap");
```

The Mapillary CDN script creates a global `mapillary` object:

```js
new mapillary.Viewer(...);
```

So you have two valid approaches:

1. **CDN/global approach** — simplest for your current plain Express app:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/mapillary-js@4.1.2/dist/mapillary.js"></script>
<script src="js/streetview.js"></script>
```

```js
const viewer = new mapillary.Viewer(options);
```

2. **ES module/bundler approach** — keep the import, but use a module-aware setup such as Vite:

```js
import { Viewer } from "mapillary-js";
```

Your current project has no bundler, so the CDN/global approach fits it best.

