You do not need a “write access token” to display Mapillary images. Viewing images through MapillaryJS requires a valid client access token or user access token; write/upload permissions are separate and unnecessary here. [Mapillary token documentation](https://mapillary.github.io/mapillary-js/docs/intro/try/)

Your token has the expected client-token format:

```text
MLY|client-id|client-token
```

To verify it:

1. Open the [Mapillary Developer Dashboard](https://www.mapillary.com/dashboard/developers).
2. Open your application.
3. Find the **Client Token** section.
4. Copy the token again using the copy button.
5. Replace the token in your test request exactly, without quotes, spaces, or line breaks.

Then test whether the token can access any public imagery:

```js
fetch(
  "https://graph.mapillary.com/images" +
    "?bbox=-79.34,43.73,-79.31,43.76" +
    "&limit=1" +
    "&fields=id" +
    "&access_token=YOUR_TOKEN",
)
  .then(async (response) => {
    console.log("Status:", response.status);
    console.log(await response.text());
  });
```

Interpretation:

- `200` with image data: your token works.
- `401`: the token is invalid.
- `403`: the token or application lacks access.
- `200` with an empty array: token works, but no matching images were found.

Since your request returned:

```text
Object with ID ... does not exist, cannot be loaded due to missing permissions
```

the most likely possibilities are that the image was removed, is unavailable to the API, or your token cannot access that particular image. It does not necessarily mean the token lacks write access.

You can also test with Mapillary’s documented sample image:

```js
viewer.moveTo("3748064795322267");
```

If that works but `339131707727137` fails, your token is valid and the specific Toronto image is unavailable through the API.

Because the token was pasted into this conversation, regenerate the client token in the Mapillary dashboard before using the project publicly.
